<p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christopher_Lee">Sir Christopher Lee</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edgar_Allan_Poe">Edgar Allan Poe</a> went way back. Or at least they went as far back as 1969, the year of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Oblong_Box_(film)"><em>The Oblong</em> <em>Box</em>,</a> a loose and lurid film adaptation of the eponymous story starring Lee and the <a href="https://www.google.com/">also Poe-associated</a> Vincent Price. Lee plays the doctor to whom a gang of grave-robbers delivers Price’s character, an Englishman disfigured in a voodoo ceremony who fakes his own death in order to escape the captivity of his brother. Whatever its entertainment value, the picture doesn’t rank especially high in the canon of Poe adaptations. Nor, for that matter, does the original story rank alongside works like <a href="https://youtu.be/7U_l854Q778">“The Pit and the Pendulum,”</a> <a href="https://youtu.be/Ju1OfKkxMHM">“The Fall of the House of Usher,”</a> <a href="https://youtu.be/KuDCqOt8LLc">“The Cask of Amontillado,”</a> or <a href="https://youtu.be/LCKYDHcd_Mw">“The Black Cat.”</a></p>
<p>You can hear Lee himself read all those macabre, imaginative stories and others besides in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLWtg8NXV0s4Y">the YouTube playlist above</a>. Some of the recordings come from&nbsp;<em>Christopher Lee Reads Edgar Allan Poe Tales of Horror</em>, the 1979 budget-label cassette previously featured here on Open Culture.&nbsp;Others include Lee’s performances of&nbsp;<a href="https://youtu.be/gO5BlmulnQE">“The Masque of the Red Death,”</a>&nbsp;<a href="https://youtu.be/Z_utA6j3Oc8">“The Tell-Tale Heart,”</a>&nbsp;<a href="https://youtu.be/RflSM4rSxEk">“Hop-Frog,”</a>&nbsp;and <a href="https://youtu.be/OtRellPps1o">“The Murders in the Rue Morgue,”</a> which is commonly credited with launching the entire genre of detective fiction.</p>
