<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>Though now most widely remembered as a horror actor, the prolific Lee proved commanding in a variety of genres: in the final decade of his life, he appeared in numerous crime pictures, adaptations of <em>The Golden Compass&nbsp;</em>and&nbsp;<em>Charlie and the Chocolate Factory</em>, and&nbsp;several <em>Star Wars</em> and <em>Lord of the Rings</em> movies.</p>
<p>Not that he’d left Poe behind. Among the last credits Lee racked up in his lifetime is <em>Extraordinary Tales</em>, from 2013, a five-story anthology film in which he narrates “The Fall of the House of Usher.” Though British, his distinctive manner and slightly <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2016/03/why-do-people-talk-funny-in-old-movies-or-the-origin-of-the-mid-atlantic-accent.html">mid-Atlantic</a> accent were as versatile on the screen as they were in readings of Poe, whose writing dates from a time when more than a few Americans still considered literature itself an import from across the pond. This Halloween, if you don’t have the hours free for a complete listen-through of the playlist do consider at least putting on <a href="https://youtu.be/BefliMlEzZ8">“The Raven.”</a>&nbsp;It remains a matter of debate among Poe enthusiasts to what extent Lee’s delivery of “nevermore” really captures the spirit of the poem, but it would be hard to imagine a more appropriate figure to preside over All Hallows’ Eve.</p>
